 "design": "R6 settling whether flat compound symmetry really covers worse than a structured truth.\n\nDesign fixed before running (2026-09-16, written 18:25 EDT). snap-r6-structured-truth scored five\ncovariance shapes at 4,000 replicates and returned 0.9357 for flat compound symmetry against 0.9463 for\nthe dominant-benchmark shape, which is about 2.7 Monte Carlo standard errors apart. That gap is large\nenough to report and too small to trust, since a reading built on 4,000 replicates has already misled us\nonce in this project, when a 0.012 corner gap vanished at 12,000. If flat really is the hard case, the\npaper should say that its own compound-symmetry sweeps are the conservative ones, and if the gap closes,\nthe shape sweep should be reported as five numbers that agree.\nThis run repeats the two cells that straddle the gap, flat compound symmetry and the dominant-benchmark\nshape, at 12,000 replicates each on the same generator and the same five interval constructions. Three\ntimes the replicates cuts the Monte Carlo standard error from 0.0039 to 0.0022, which resolves a gap of\n0.0106 if the gap is real. Seed 20260982 keeps the draws distinct from the first sweep.\n",
